WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.439
In Lapu-Lapu City, Cebu, the notices a family needs come from different offices.

00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:08.906
MECO posts brownouts, often as images with no text.

00:00:08.906 --> 00:00:11.489
MCWD posts water cuts on its own site.

00:00:11.489 --> 00:00:14.345
DepEd and the city post class suspensions.

00:00:14.345 --> 00:00:18.832
Miss one, and you lose a day of water, or the food in your fridge.

00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:26.650
Abiso reads every official notice once, and turns it into an event you can check, ask about, and hand to any agent.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:30.436
This is the live prototype, running on Cloud Run.

00:00:30.436 --> 00:00:35.107
The feed holds 338 disruption events compiled from 201 official posts.

00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:43.048
Right now, 4 notices are in effect, including an MCWD water interruption in Barangay Basak, for a mainline leak repair.

00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:48.987
Every card links to the official post it came from, and names the model that compiled it.

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:51.735
Most people here do not search.

00:00:51.735 --> 00:00:52.316
They ask.

00:00:52.316 --> 00:00:54.446
So here is a question in Cebuano:

00:00:54.446 --> 00:00:57.610
is there a water interruption in Basak right now?

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:06.778
A Gemini agent, built with Google's Agent Development Kit, hands the question to its water specialist, which can only read the stored notices.

00:01:06.778 --> 00:01:10.393
The answer comes back in Cebuano, with the link to MCWD.

00:01:15.443 --> 00:01:16.423
Now in Filipino:

00:01:16.423 --> 00:01:18.320
are classes suspended tomorrow?

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.748
There is no official notice, so Abiso says exactly that.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:26.583
It never guesses, because a wrong answer about classes is worse than no answer.

00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:41.555
A broader question, about Pusok this week, goes to all five specialists:

00:01:41.555 --> 00:01:43.992
power, water, classes, roads and weather.

00:01:43.992 --> 00:01:48.687
The coordinator merges what they found into one short answer for that barangay.

00:02:07.657 --> 00:02:09.556
The same events can be browsed.

00:02:09.556 --> 00:02:13.111
Here are past brownouts in Basak, each from a MECO notice.

00:02:13.111 --> 00:02:15.501
Many of those notices were only images.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:21.997
Gemini read each image once, when the post was compiled, so answering questions later costs nothing extra.

00:02:23.297 --> 00:02:27.233
Other agents get the same answers, with no model call at all.

00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:34.591
The REST API returns the events as JSON, and the same functions are exposed as MCP tools and as an A2A agent card.

00:02:34.591 --> 00:02:38.657
Any assistant can ask Abiso, instead of guessing from old news.

00:02:39.163 --> 00:02:43.359
Under the hood, Cloud Scheduler calls the service every three hours.

00:02:43.359 --> 00:02:54.588
It captures new posts from MECO, MCWD, DepEd and PAGASA, compiles only the new ones with Gemini on Vertex AI, merges extensions and cancellations, and stores the result in Firestore.

00:02:54.588 --> 00:03:00.573
The first refresh captured forty-seven posts and compiled thirty-three new ones, for eight cents.

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:04.725
We measured it on fifty test posts, locked before any model saw them.

00:03:04.725 --> 00:03:13.400
Gemini got the type of disruption right every time, the start time within an hour ninety-two percent of the time, and the barangays with ninety-four percent overlap.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.977
Status is still the weakest field, and we say so.

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:18.740
Abiso.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:23.915
Official notices for Lapu-Lapu City, compiled once, answered anywhere.

00:03:23.915 --> 00:03:27.537
It is live now, and the code is public on GitHub.

